  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="map" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>S.1 · terminate each secret where it is needed</span>
        <h2>“Put it in a Secret” is only the first step.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="credential-map">
        <article><span>provider token</span><strong>broker only</strong><p>Owner-only file → destination-bound native HTTP injection.</p><code>github-comment-token</code></article>
        <article><span>chat token</span><strong>gateway only</strong><p>Environment variable named by gateway configuration.</p><code>DEKOPOND_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN</code></article>
        <article><span>telemetry token</span><strong>each exporter</strong><p>Standard OpenTelemetry headers environment variable.</p><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code></article>
        <article><span>ChatGPT subscription</span><strong>writable gateway state</strong><p>Seed once, rotate in place, never restore a stale copy.</p><code>chatgpt-auth.json</code></article>
        <article><span>provider storage key</span><strong>broker only</strong><p>Operator-owned namespace key retained separately from provider data.</p><code>storage-key.yaml</code></article>
      </div>

      <p>No secret belongs in the container image, agent catalog, Cedar policy, model prompt, provider component, command line, trace attribute, or durable audit payload. The storage key also stays out of the gateway and provider guest.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="provider" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>S.2 · provider authority</span>
        <h2>A broker token is a file, then a destination-bound value.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="secret-path">
        <div><small>secret manager or kubectl</small><strong>Kubernetes Secret</strong><span>provisioning object</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">project →</i>
        <div><small>init container</small><strong>owner-only regular file</strong><span><code>0600</code> · UID 65532 · one link</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">broker loads →</i>
        <div><small>constraint set</small><strong>symbolic credential</strong><span>capability + allowed hosts</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">native client injects →</i>
        <div><small>one HTTP request</small><strong>Authorization header</strong><span>provider never sees value</span></div>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment"># broker-credentials.yaml — materialized, never committed</span>
apiVersion: dekopon.dev/broker-credentials/v1alpha1
credentials:
  - name: github-comment-token
    kind: bearerToken
    scheme: Bearer
    destinations: [api.github.com]
    secret: [REDACTED]</code></pre>

      <p>Startup proves that every constraint set selecting this credential permits only destinations in its binding. Guest code cannot provide its own <code>Authorization</code> header and cannot inspect the injected one.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="gateway" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>S.3 · ordinary runtime credentials</span>
        <h2>Gateway configuration names variables, never values.</h2>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment"># dekopond.yaml</span>
transports:
  - name: workspace-slack
    kind: slackSocketMode
    appTokenEnv: DEKOPOND_SLACK_APP_TOKEN
    botTokenEnv: DEKOPOND_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN

models:
  - name: hosted-reasoning
    kind: openaiCompatible
    endpoint: https://api.openai.com/v1
    model: gpt-5
    apiKeyEnv: OPENAI_API_KEY</code></pre>

      <p>The chart can use ordinary <code>secretKeyRef</code> environment entries for these values. The same rule covers Discord, Telegram, WhatsApp, and optional image-generator credentials: configuration names an environment variable, never its value. A field containing a token instead of a valid variable name is a startup failure.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="telemetry" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>S.4 · exporter authentication</span>
        <h2>Telemetry credentials follow the OpenTelemetry standard.</h2>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment"># Pod environment, sourced from a Secret</span>
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=Authorization=Basic%20[REDACTED],organization=default,stream-name=dekopon</code></pre>

      <p>The telemetry configuration contains the endpoint, transport, service name, timeout, and payload choice. The ingest credential stays in the standard headers variable so it never enters parsed YAML, process arguments, span fields, or logs.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="chatgpt" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>S.5 · the rotating exception</span>
        <h2>A ChatGPT credential must be seeded once.</h2>
        <p>The device login requires a human with a browser. Its refresh token then rotates whenever the gateway refreshes access, invalidating the previous copy.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="chatgpt-lifecycle">
        <div><span>1 · local machine</span><strong><code>dekopon auth chatgpt login</code></strong><small>human completes device flow</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">export once →</i>
        <div><span>2 · Kubernetes Secret</span><strong>seed material</strong><small>not a durable backup</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">copy only if absent →</i>
        <div><span>3 · persistent volume</span><strong>live writable credential</strong><small>gateway rotates token in place</small></div>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment"># Local export to a Kubernetes Secret manifest</span>
dekopon auth chatgpt export \
  --expose-credential \
  --namespace dekopon | kubectl apply -f -</code></pre>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>directory</span><h3>Writable parent required</h3><p>Refresh writes a temporary sibling, synchronizes it, and renames it over the credential.</p></article>
        <article><span>volume</span><h3>Persistent storage required</h3><p>An <code>emptyDir</code> would disappear on reschedule and reintroduce the stale seed.</p></article>
        <article><span>guard</span><h3>Copy only when absent</h3><p>Any existing destination means “already seeded.” Restart must never overwrite the live token.</p></article>
        <article><span>writer</span><h3>One refresh lock</h3><p>Version 0.10.0 coordinates rotation across processes with an advisory lock beside the credential file. The chart still runs one gateway replica because the broker and audit path require it.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>The exported copy goes stale by design.</strong>
        <p>After the first refresh, the persistent volume holds the only live refresh token. Restoring the original Secret over it breaks a working gateway. Re-seeding must be an explicit destructive operation after a fresh login and export.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="storage-key" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>S.6 · durable provider namespaces</span>
        <h2>Retain the provider data and its key separately.</h2>
        <p>Version 0.10.0 can give a provider broker-owned JSONL or durable-file storage. The namespace key turns authority and scope into opaque storage names; the provider receives no host path or key bytes.</p>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment"># storage-key.yaml — operator-owned, 0600, never committed</span>
apiVersion: dekopon.dev/storage-key/v1alpha1
key: [64 LOWERCASE HEX CHARACTERS]</code></pre>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>lifecycle</span><h3>The chart never creates the key</h3><p>An operator-managed Secret supplies it. Uninstalling Dekopon must not delete the only key that can reopen retained data.</p></article>
        <article><span>mount</span><h3>Broker only</h3><p>The init container copies the key into broker-only memory as a <code>0600</code> file. The gateway mounts neither key nor provider storage.</p></article>
        <article><span>scope</span><h3>No path reaches the guest</h3><p>The host binds storage to provider, capability, interface, chat scope, quotas, and one invocation.</p></article>
        <article><span>limit</span><h3>Not content encryption</h3><p>The key protects namespace derivation and commitments. Dekopon does not claim provider text is encrypted at rest.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p>A retained claim with a missing or changed key fails closed. Back up, rotate, and recover the key as a separate operator procedure; do not treat a Kubernetes Secret projection as the only durable copy.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="external-secrets" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>S.7 · provisioning versus materialization</span>
        <h2>External Secrets can deliver a value, not a broker-ready file.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="eso-chain">
        <div><span>1Password</span><strong>vault item</strong></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>External Secrets</span><strong>ClusterSecretStore</strong></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>namespace</span><strong>Kubernetes Secret</strong></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="eso-boundary"><span>Dekopon chart</span><strong>init copy + file checks</strong></div>
      </div>

      <p>External Secrets solves how a value reaches the cluster. It does not change projected-volume symlinks, ownership, group readability, or the ChatGPT rotation lifecycle. Dekopon’s init container remains the materialization boundary.</p>

      {{ docsCta("Open the current 1Password and External Secrets guide", "https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon/blob/main/docs/1password-eso.md", "See the latest bootstrap runbook, store configuration, deletion caveats, mount-shape failures, and distinction between provisioning and safe file materialization.") }}
    </section>

